The memory safety holy grail is a way to *simply and seamlessly combine borrowing with reference counting* without the usual problems and symptoms: panics, deadlocks, undefined behavior, complexity, and various kinds of extra run-time overhead (cloning, hashing, extra RC adjustments, tracing, etc).


As far we we know, it's impossible to blend those two approaches without those problems. [# TODO: Some note here]


For example, we can't compile [this Swift program](https://swiftfiddle.com/3xo46ohjl5gbbgthivf5tylvty?c=H4sIAAAAAAAAA4VUTW%2FbMAz9K5wvSwDPxXbYwUDbQxBgBYqeAuzQFIUiM7FQRQokOalRZL99lOQPOekwH2xJJB%2Bpx0d%2FZDwrM%2BtMwx08as6c0KqEX8zWbCMRPtYK6DkyA%2B8lPCg37ttuf16rteKSWQuPeESZxqBywgm0JTwv%2FbJ9GW1OyGAYs67opHOIb6GEm11j5P%2BKnfe5%2FWNRbos%2BGG6HWi48AhSZwzfawo38YtsoDjUzR7TuibnG4FKh2bWz1%2BAes%2BbwGrGJkFjgHL7deW7SarbMMbk0RpvZOmuU2B8k7ikMq3U2H9ImXHroCHBzA0VRTIwxT48v0YGoLtojNS%2BHjo7HvGYWV8zs0JWwGDf3V7x3iPkUKZ8icK1ssxdqN8G67oOoiGJRXZwSMh3T%2B%2BI8SUH2ZDclqpPtIi3oz0If2lS6J2RvUW%2BdR%2BTufqTkwFy90j3AoKmXLksQAWeSN5I5fMJ313uQDCZkbDT193RBhpeHPSBGNoM0%2Bvi%2BxP9oI6lCU%2B2qwQA%2Fk37ayjh0%2BZUEA7Y9CcfrzjahtUvN6QQKq%2FcezqXXmZdeeA9bIvCrwWDxN%2FOuWmHfMLFNY4rIMXy5BSXoR9AhuBpbj2FpaCQwKY44NpwQfGY%2FT14L%2FjJxJp%2B7qkkeL6mewkD8NsLR2Gol2xCaw6ZxYJBVtrstMFWBptTU%2BZoqtymCzxiaMqT8bMojcISbp%2BFjZV6en0qDpwoIqRKEc78cFhsq%2FS1tiSKSyyurV36WZ0f6Z%2F8sfhTfs%2FNf7JUfd8EFAAA%3D) without extra run-time overhead or odd symptoms: [#grailgoal] [# The "extra overhead" in this program happens in the `switch entity.chaseTarget` part, which is trying to obtain a lock to borrow the contents, similar to Rust's RefCell. See [this](https://www.swift.org/blog/swift-5-exclusivity/) for the concept and [Exclusivity.cpp](https://github.com/swiftlang/swift/blob/main/stdlib/public/runtime/Exclusivity.cpp#L161) for how it works under the hood. It's actually O(n) checking at run-time. One user even reported a [25% overhead](https://mjtsai.com/blog/2019/02/05/swift-5-exclusivity-enforcement/#comment-3815897) for a section of their code.]

```swift
func continueChase(level: Level, entity: Entity) {
  // Run-time overhead here to get an exclusive lock
  switch entity.chaseTarget {
    // Get borrow reference to contained ChaseTarget
    case .some(let chaseTarget):
      if chaseTarget.target != nil { // If they're still alive
        if let tile = level.tiles[entity.loc] {
          // Writes only tile, but reads entity and other things
          let speed = level.harvestNatureEnergy(tile, entity)
          entity.loc = calculateNextLocation(chaseTarget, speed)
        }
      }
      break
    case .none:
      break
  }
  // Run-time overhead here to release exclusive lock
}
```

`let chaseTarget` is a borrow reference (similar to Rust), so Swift inserts an implicit lock (similar to `RefCell`) and unlock, mostly because it can't guarantee that `level.harvestNatureEnergy` doesn't modify the `entity`.


That run-time check is the only way Swift knows how to protect against cases like in [this other program](https://gist.github.com/Verdagon/5772534141c20ac36c67704b7de115b0) that halts at run-time:

```swift
func maintenance(_ ship: Spaceship) {
  // Invalid attempt to change ship.v, program halts
  ship.v = Variant.navigation(Navigation(antenna: true))
}
func foo(_ ship: Spaceship) {
  switch ship.v { // Locks and borrows ship.v
    case .navigation(let navigation):
      break
    case .engine(let engine): // engine is a borrow reference
      maintenance(ship)
      let warp_coils = engine.warp_coils // Invalid read
      print("Warp coils:", warp_coils)
  }
}
```

...because it doesn't know how to detect this problem at compile time.


Like I said, whenever a language tries to make reference counting work with borrowing, it inevitably encounters problems, like the overhead and run-time crashes here, or other symptoms depending on the language.

Other languages fare no better. Even my very own Vale hasn't succeeded here!


To this day, nobody knows how to make a compiler smart enough to avoid the overhead and catch the failures at compile-time.


On first glance, these may seem like trivial snippets in a trivial corner of language design. But I have reason to believe that if someone can solve these, then they've actually solved *an entire class of problems,* 


For example, if we could figure this out, then I believe [this Rust program](https://play.rust-lang.org/?version=stable&mode=debug&edition=2024&gist=00064b022025b7ab2ba3b165c9bee5e3) (equivalent to the first Swift one):

```rs
fn continue_chase(level: &Rc<RefCell<Level>>, entity: &Rc<RefCell<Entity>>) -> bool {
  let entity_read = entity.borrow();
  if let Some(ref chase_target_ref_1) = entity_read.chase_target {
    if chase_target_ref_1.target.upgrade().is_some() {
      let level_mut = level.borrow_mut();
      if let Some(tile) = level_mut.tiles.get(&entity_read.loc) {
        drop(entity_read);
        let speed = level_mut.harvest_nature_energy(tile, entity); // Modifies only tile
        let mut entity_mut = entity.borrow_mut(); // Re-fetch entity ref
        if let Some(ref chase_target_ref_2) = entity_mut.chase_target { // Re-check
          entity_mut.loc = calculate_next_location(chase_target_ref_2, speed);
          return true;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return false;
}
```

...could be reduced to something like this:

```rs
fn continue_chase(level: &Rc<Level>, entity: &Rc<Entity>) -> bool {
  if let Some(ref chase_target) = entity.chase_target {
    if chase_target.target.upgrade().is_some() {
      if let Some(tile) = level.tiles.get(&entity.loc) {
        let speed = level.harvest_nature_energy(tile, entity); // Modifies only tile
        entity.loc = calculate_next_location(chase_target, speed);
        return true;
      }
    }
  }
  return false;
}
```

...which is faster, doesn't risk run-time crashes anymore, and is (in my opinion) simpler than the other usual workarounds. [# The main workarounds are [cloning](https://play.rust-lang.org/?version=stable&mode=debug&edition=2021&gist=16679b086ea71ae631816359fd0788ab) (risking heap allocation, defeating the purpose) or [hashing/IDs](https://play.rust-lang.org/?version=stable&mode=debug&edition=2021&gist=cf2c253d948f6502d6991027dfc500aa) (which has unnecessary hashing/checks and often violates encapsulation).]


If we could figure this out, we would be one step closer to the ever-mythical language that has the ever-unreachable trio of safety, speed, and simplicity. [# As someone who designs memory safety approaches for fun, this has been haunting my dreams for _years._] High-level languages (Swift, C#, etc.) could be faster, and borrowing languages (Rust, Mojo, etc.) could better handle intrusive data structures, observers, graphs, back-references, dependency references, delegates, any all sorts of patterns that borrowing has trouble with today.


